{"brief":"The user asked verbatim: “Which famous chefs trained in which other chefs' kitchens? Draw the lineage tree.” Build a beautiful, highly legible culinary lineage report led by ONE large directed network/tree visualization. An arrow runs from mentor / kitchen leader to trainee / alumnus. Use r9f3f674141e as the main edge list: one row per documented chef-to-chef kitchen relationship, with mentor, trainee, lineage branch, kitchen, relationship, period, number of independent source mentions found, source title and source URL. Show all 56 curated links. Treat “trained” broadly but accurately as actual apprenticeship, stage, or formative employment in the senior chef’s kitchen—not mere influence, friendship, school attendance, or TV inspiration. Make labels distinguish relationship type; tooltips/details should show kitchen, period, and source. Where the same chef has multiple mentors, preserve the network rather than forcing a strict single-parent tree.

The central finding: modern fine dining is a densely cross-linked apprenticeship network, not a single pedigree. Strong hubs in the curated set are Fernand Point/La Pyramide, the Roux–Koffmann–Marco Pierre White–Gordon Ramsay chain, Ferran Adrià/elBulli, and Thomas Keller/The French Laundry. Particularly striking bridges include René Redzepi working at both elBulli and The French Laundry; Grant Achatz passing through Charlie Trotter’s, The French Laundry, and elBulli; Alain Ducasse learning in kitchens led by Michel Guérard, Roger Vergé, and Alain Chapel; and Gordon Ramsay working for Albert Roux, Marco Pierre White, Pierre Koffmann, Guy Savoy, and Joël Robuchon. Fernand Point’s La Pyramide connects to Paul Bocuse, Alain Chapel, the Troisgros brothers, and Louis Outhier; Paul Bocuse also apprenticed with Eugénie Brazier. Ferran Adrià’s elBulli connects to René Redzepi, Massimo Bottura, José Andrés, Joan Roca, Andoni Luis Aduriz, Grant Achatz, Albert Adrià, and Paco Pérez.

Use r0285ad2ba02 for an exact branch-summary view, already aggregated (do not recount rows): British brigade 20 links, French foundations 19, elBulli/Spanish avant-garde 8, US fine dining 5, global offshoots 4. Total is 56 documented links. This is an illustrative, researched map of famous chefs—not a claim to list every famous chef worldwide. The underlying searches scanned thousands of web results across biographies, Michelin Guide, World’s 50 Best, Britannica, major newspapers, and specialist chef profiles; edges were retained when the extraction had at least two source mentions, then curated for recognizability and coherent branches. Source-mention count measures corroboration in this scan, not importance. Some source rows are stronger than others, so display links and allow the reader to judge them. Use tasteful editorial colors by lineage branch, directional arrows, clear spacing, and a companion searchable table or cards beneath the hero visualization. Make the visualization beautiful and responsive.","result_set_ids":["r9f3f674141e","r0285ad2ba02"],"title":"The Great-Chef Kitchen Lineage","prev_report_token":null}
